        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:Hn">Hn</ref> 92.5</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="Hn 92.5" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="608" ed="#liebermann"/>Murdritus homo dicebatur antiquitus cuius interfector nesciebatur, ubicunque uel quomodocunque<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="1" target="#Hn_92-5.lnote.oe.1"/> esset<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="2" target="#Hn_92-5.lnote.oe.2"/> inuentus; nunc adiectum est: licet sciatur<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="3" target="#Hn_92-5.lnote.oe.3"/>, quis murdrum fecerit, si non habeatur intra VII dies<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="a" target="#Hn_92-5.lnote.oe.a"/>.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 608 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Hn_92-5.lnote.oe.a" n="a" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">608</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">wie</hi> 91, 1. 92, 3; 9<hi rend="italic">b</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">as</hi> 91, 1. 92, 3; 9<hi rend="italic">b</hi></seg></note>
